WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:05.210
The Game of Thrones finale left a lot of viewers
asking questions, and now some of them have

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:08.069
been answered, thanks to the release of the
episode's script.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:13.040
Here's a look at some of the most surprising
small details uncovered in its pages.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:17.680
After the Game of Thrones finale aired, fans
hurried online to try and find an answer to

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:21.930
one huge lingering question about the destruction
of the Iron Throne.

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:26.160
Did Drogon intentionally destroy the Iron
Throne because he recognized it as the reason

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.430
for Daenerys' downfall?

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:30.660
Or was he just a big dumb animal lashing out?

00:00:30.660 --> 00:00:34.070
Turns out that big dragon is just a big, dumb
animal.

00:00:34.070 --> 00:00:38.320
In the script, showrunners David Benioff and
D.B. Weiss wrote that Drogon wanted to burn

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.499
the world, saying,

00:00:39.499 --> 00:00:44.739
"We look over Jon's shoulder as the fire sweeps
toward the throne not the target of Drogon's

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:48.320
wrath, just a dumb bystander caught up in
the conflagration."

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:53.240
So for anyone looking for some kind of meaning
in these events: there isn't any!

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:57.400
One thing that chapped a lot of hides was
the fact that we never got a sense of what

00:00:57.420 --> 00:01:02.510
Daenerys was feeling or thinking after she
went on her rampage in the penultimate episode.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:06.970
The finale script unfortunately doesn't offer
any insight either: from the moment Jon enters

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:11.670
the throne room in her final scene, the script
doesn't give any hint of Dany's point of view,

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.040
focusing entirely on Jon Snow.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.500
Fans who were upset at Daenerys' lack of dialogue
during the siege of King's Landing probably

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:22.740
won't be any happier knowing the dragon queen
didn't even get an inner monologue while she

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:24.680
was being stabbed to death.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:29.680
Over the course of the show, Daenerys certainly
acquired plenty of lofty titles.

00:01:29.700 --> 00:01:36.040
"You stand in the presence of Daenerys Stormborn
of House Targaryen, rightful heir to the Iron

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:38.730
Throne, rightful queen of the Andals and the
First Men..."

00:01:38.730 --> 00:01:41.100
Like... a lot of them.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:47.380
"...Protector of the Seven Kingdoms, the Mother
of Dragons, the Khaleesi of the great Grass

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.980
Sea, the Unburnt, the Breaker of Chains."

00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:55.430
But while many fans were calling her the Mad
Queen at the end, just as her father before

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:59.730
her was the Mad King, the script actually
gave her a more unexpected title during the

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:02.040
finale: "Her Satanic Majesty."

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:03.040
Yep!

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.170
While describing the scene where Dany lords
over her conquered city, the script read:

00:02:07.170 --> 00:02:13.000
"For a moment, Drogon's unfolding wings spread
behind her back, an unsettling image.

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:15.680
Her Satanic Majesty's Request."

00:02:15.680 --> 00:02:21.370
That's actually a reference to the 1967 Rolling
Stones album Their Satanic Majesties Request,

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:24.160
which contains a lot of trippy imagery.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.040
One of the most haunting sequences in the
Game of Thrones finale is Tyrion's long walk

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:31.180
through the ruins of King's Landing.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:35.380
Benioff and Weiss deliberately packed this
part of the script with cultural and historical

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.500
references to give it added oomph.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:42.710
The focus on Tyrion's face, for instance,
was inspired by the 2015 Holocaust drama Son

00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:43.710
of Saul.

00:02:43.710 --> 00:02:44.710
The script read,

00:02:44.710 --> 00:02:49.050
"We follow him Son of Saul style, tight on
his face as he passes through the gates."

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:54.410
And a few lines later, the directions reference
another tragedy from World War II, the atomic

00:02:54.410 --> 00:02:57.180
bombing of Hiroshima, Japan, saying,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:03:00.950
"Human silhouettes on the dragon-scorched
ground where the ashes have blown away, the

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.440
negative image of the Hiroshima silhouettes."

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:08.960
One place the script does provide important
nuance is that weird council meeting where

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:13.450
the heads of the noble families of Westeros
somehow ended up electing Bran as King.

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:17.830
It seemed a bit random to many fans, but the
script provides some much needed explanation

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:19.910
as to why the council voted that way.

00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:21.971
Edmure Tully, Bran's uncle, is

00:03:21.971 --> 00:03:26.950
"[...] miffed he wasn't given serious consideration,
but he expects he'll have influence at court

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.080
if his crippled nephew is ruling."

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:34.740
Meanwhile, Gendry is, quote, "happy to go
along," while Yara Greyjoy has apparently,

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:37.080
"[...] heard that her brother died defending
Bran.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:39.590
She knows this choice would make Theon happy."

00:03:39.590 --> 00:03:43.050
Finally, Brienne of Tarth is, quote, "a Stark
loyalist."

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.569
One person who doesn't get a lot of development?

00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:50.099
That random new Prince of Dorne, who is such
a nonentity that even the script doesn't bother

00:03:50.099 --> 00:03:51.620
giving him a name.

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.320
Sorry, whoever you are!

00:03:53.320 --> 00:03:57.989
If you thought it was weird that Bran became
king, well, Weiss and Benioff agreed, as the

00:03:57.989 --> 00:04:01.709
script itself noted more than once how odd
this plot twist was.

00:04:01.709 --> 00:04:03.709
Remember when Tyrion offered him the crown?

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:05.650
"Will you wear the crown?"

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:08.950
"Why do you think I came all this way?"

00:04:08.950 --> 00:04:10.590
In the script, it then said,

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:15.239
"A strange response but Tyrion has come to
expect strange answers from Bran."

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:20.310
Then, in the small council meeting, after
asking where Drogon is, Bran drops this:

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:22.730
"Perhaps I can find him."

00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:24.940
In the script, this is followed by a note
reading

00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:26.410
"That's weird.

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:28.139
But so is the new king."

00:04:28.139 --> 00:04:29.139
Also weird?

00:04:29.139 --> 00:04:33.470
The fact that Sam is wearing Grandmaester
robes even though he never completed his training.

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:38.540
But the script makes it clear that he is indeed
a Grandmaester despite his limited qualifications.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:41.780
When Sam enters the small council chamber,
the script notes,

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:45.510
"[...] he wears the Grandmaester robes and
a chain with a single healing link."

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.160
At least someone in this show got a happy
ending.

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:52.870
At the end of the show, Jon Snow was exiled
to the far north as he rejoins the Night's

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:53.870
Watch.

00:04:53.870 --> 00:04:57.790
We end the series with Jon and his wildling
friends wandering past the wall.

00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:01.780
Considering there seemed to be no reason to
even have a Night's Watch any more, that led

00:05:01.780 --> 00:05:05.810
some fans to think he was taking his place
as the King Beyond the Wall.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:10.169
But the script seems to suggest he's just
a regular schmoe, as it unequivocally calls

00:05:10.169 --> 00:05:12.780
him a "Night's Watchman" rather than a King.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:17.669
Considering the fate of most monarchs on the
show, though, that's probably for the best.

